  ],
  "span": 0.45,
  "answered_by": "It is Dürer himself at 28, painted frontally with a blessing-like hand, in the way contemporary images showed Christ (Wikipedia; Alte Pinakothek)"
 },
 "facts": [
  {
   "claim": "Dürer's father, Albrecht Dürer the Elder, was a goldsmith in Nuremberg",
   "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albrecht_D%C3%BCrer"
  },
  {
   "claim": "In 1484, as a boy of 12 or 13, Dürer drew himself in silverpoint from a mirror (Albertina, Vienna); the inscription added later reads 'This I drew myself from a mirror in the year 1484, when I was still a child'",
   "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Self-Portrait_at_the_Age_of_13 ; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albrecht_D%C3%BCrer"
  },
  {
   "claim": "Self-portrait with a Bandage, pen drawing, 1491–92, University Library Erlangen-Nuremberg: his hand pressed to his face; after his apprenticeship with Wolgemut (1486–90) Dürer travelled as a journeyman from 1490",
   "source": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Selbstbildnis_mit_Binde_(Duerer).jpg ; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albrecht_D%C3%BCrer"
  },
  {
   "claim": "1493: Portrait of the Artist Holding a Thistle (Louvre), his first painted self-portrait, probably sent to his fiancée Agnes Frey in Nuremberg; the plant (eryngium) is often read as a sign of fidelity; he married her in July 1494",
   "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portrait_of_the_Artist_Holding_a_Thistle ; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albrecht_D%C3%BCrer"
  },
  {
   "claim": "1498: Self-portrait at 26 (Prado), painted after his first trip to Italy, in extravagant Italian-style clothes and silk gloves, raising himself to the social position he thought an artist deserved",
   "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Self-Portrait_(D%C3%BCrer,_Madrid)"
  },
  {
   "claim": "1500: Self-Portrait at Twenty-Eight (Alte Pinakothek, Munich): frontal, symmetrical, the hand as if blessing; it resembles contemporary depictions of Christ, read as 'stunning arrogance or blasphemy'; a frontal pose was exceptional for a secular portrait",
   "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Self-Portrait_(D%C3%BCrer,_Munich)"
  },
  {
   "claim": "In a letter from Venice (1506, to Willibald Pirckheimer) Dürer wrote that there he was a gentleman, at home only a parasite",
   "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Self-Portrait_(D%C3%BCrer,_Madrid) ; https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albrecht_D%C3%BCrer"
  }
 ],
 "caption": "These self-portraits show a goldsmith's son turning himself into Christ.\n\n1484: still a boy, Albrecht Dürer draws himself in silverpoint from a mirror. Decades later he adds a note: \"This I drew myself from a mirror in the year 1484, when I was still a child.\"\nAbout 1491: a young journeyman, his hand pressed to his face.\n1493: at 22 he paints himself holding a thistle, probably for Agnes Frey, the bride waiting for him in Nuremberg.\n1498: back from his first trip to Italy, he wears silk gloves and Italian finery, dressed like a gentleman.\n1500: at 28 he faces us head-on, symmetrical, his hand raised as if in blessing. No secular portrait looked like this: it was how painters showed Christ. Arrogance or blasphemy? Historians still argue.\n\nFrom Venice in 1506 he wrote to his friend Pirckheimer that there he was a gentleman, at home only a parasite.\n\nIt hangs in the Alte Pinakothek, in Munich.\n\n👁️ Which one would you hang on your wall?",
 "caption_short": "These self-portraits show a goldsmith's son turning himself into Christ. Dürer, 1484 → 1500. 👁️",
 "youtube_title": "Is this Christ, or him? Albrecht Dürer's self-portraits, 1484–1500",
